The Select component placeholder "Select an option" is hardcoded and cannot be customized per field. This reduces flexibility and makes it difficult to provide context-specific placeholder text.

Consider adding a `placeholder` property to the field configuration that can be passed through to the SelectValue component, similar to how the Input and Textarea components handle placeholders.
```suggestion
      const {
        value: selectValue,
        onChange: selectOnChange,
        placeholder,
        ...selectProps
      } = fieldProps;
      return (
        <Select value={selectValue} onValueChange={selectOnChange} {...selectProps}>
          <SelectTrigger>
            <SelectValue
              placeholder={placeholder ?? schema?.placeholder ?? 'Select an option'}
            />
```

The conditional field logic doesn't handle the case when the watched field value is `undefined` or `null`. This could cause unexpected behavior during initial form render or when fields are cleared.

For example, if `watchValue` is `undefined`, the condition `condition.in && !condition.in.includes(watchValue)` might not behave as expected. Consider adding explicit checks for `undefined` and `null` values to make the conditional logic more robust.
```suggestion
                  if (
                    condition.in &&
                    watchValue !== undefined &&
                    watchValue !== null &&
                    !condition.in.includes(watchValue)
                  ) {
```
